{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request,json\nbase='https://huggingface.co/api/datasets/amanutej/trustworthy-biology-agents-traces/tree/main/'\nfor q in ['bix-12-q4','bix-12-q5','bix-12-q6']:\n dirs=json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(base+f'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/{q}?recursive=false&expand=true',timeout=30))\n root=dirs[0]['path']\n sub=json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(base+root+'?recursive=true&expand=true',timeout=30))\n files=[it['path'] for it in sub if it['type']=='file' and it['path'].endswith('/artifacts/trace.md')]\n print('\\\\n===',q,root)\n print(files)\n for f in files[:1]:\n  raw=f'https://huggingface.co/datasets/amanutej/trustworthy-biology-agents-traces/resolve/main/{f}'\n  txt=urllib.request.urlopen(raw,timeout=30).read().decode()\n  print(txt[:5000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request,json\nbase='https://huggingface.co/api/datasets/amanutej/trustworthy-biology-agents-traces/tree/main/'\nfor q in ['bix-12-q4','bix-12-q5','bix-12-q6']:\n dirs=json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(base+f'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/{q}?recursive=false&expand=true',timeout=30))\n root=dirs[0]['path']\n sub=json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(base+root+'?recursive=true&expand=true',timeout=30))\n files=[it['path'] for it in sub if it['type']=='file' and it['path'].endswith('/artifacts/trace.md')]\n print('\\\\n===',q,root)\n print(files)\n for f in files[:1]:\n  raw=f'https://huggingface.co/datasets/amanutej/trustworthy-biology-agents-traces/resolve/main/{f}'\n  txt=urllib.request.urlopen(raw,timeout=30).read().decode()\n  print(txt[:5000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n=== bix-12-q4 traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q4/2026-06-05__01-30-41\n['traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q4/2026-06-05__01-30-41/bix-12-q4__dbuZff5/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q4/2026-06-05__01-30-41/bix-12-q4__uxyAqwL/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q4/2026-06-05__01-30-41/bix-12-q4__wViamce/artifacts/trace.md']\n# Trace: Mann-Whitney U for parsimony informative site percentages (animals vs fungi)\n\n## Objective\nQuantify and compare the percentage of parsimony-informative (PI) sites in the\nsingle-copy ortholog group (SCOG) protein alignments of the Animals clade\nversus the Fungi clade, reporting the Mann-Whitney U test statistic.\n\n## Data inspected\n- `/app/data/scogs_animals.zip` — 241 MSA files (`*.faa.mafft`) plus 241\n  unaligned `*.faa` files. MSAs contain sequences from up to 4 species\n  (`Animals_Cele`, `Animals_Ggal`, `Animals_Mmus`, `Animals_Xlae`).\n- `/app/data/scogs_fungi.zip` — 255 MSA files (`*.faa.mafft`) plus 255\n  unaligned `*.faa` files. MSAs contain sequences from up to 4 species\n  (`Fungi_Ncra`, `Fungi_Scer`, `Fungi_Spom`, `Fungi_Umay`).\n\nSequence-count distribution per alignment:\n- Animals: 70 files with 1 seq, 71 with 2, 78 with 3, 22 with 4\n- Fungi:   0 with 1, 6 with 2, 38 with 3, 211 with 4\n\nThe `.faa.mafft` files are MAFFT multiple sequence alignments — the appropriate\ninput for site-wise statistics. Each file is the alignment of one BUSCO\northolog (file name `<id>at2759.faa.mafft`, where `2759` is the eukaryote\ntaxon).\n\n## Approach\n\n### Definition of a parsimony-informative site\nA column of a multiple sequence alignment is parsimony-informative if it\ncontains at least two distinct character states each present in at least two\nsequences. Gap characters (`-`) and ambiguity codes (`X`, `?`, `.`, `*`) are\nexcluded from the state count. The PI percentage of an alignment is\n`100 * (# PI columns) / (alignment length)`.\n\n### Pipeline\n1. Unzip both `scogs_*.zip` archives.\n2. For every `*.faa.mafft` file, parse the FASTA, compute the PI percentage\n   using the rule above.\n3. Collect the two distributions (one PI% per orthologous group) and run\n   `scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu(animals, fungi, alternative='two-sided')`.\n4. Cross-check the U statistic and p-value with R's `wilcox.test`.\n\n### Code (Python)\n```python\nfrom Bio import SeqIO\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom scipy.stats import mannwhitneyu\nimport numpy as np, glob, os\n\ndef parsimony_informative_pct(fasta_path):\n    seqs = [str(r.seq).upper() for r in SeqIO.parse(fasta_path, 'fasta')]\n    if not seqs: return None\n    L = len(seqs[0]); pi = 0\n    for i in range(L):\n        chars = [s[i] for s in seqs if s[i] not in ('-','X','?','.','*')]\n        counts = Counter(chars)\n        if sum(1 for v in counts.values() if v >= 2) >= 2:\n            pi += 1\n    return 100.0 * pi / L\n\ndef gather(folder):\n    return {os.path.basename(p).replace('.faa.mafft',''):\n            parsimony_informative_pct(p)\n            for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(folder, '*.faa.mafft')))}\n\nanimals = gather('animals'); fungi = gather('fungi')\na = list(animals.values()); f = list(fungi.values())\nu, p = mannwhitneyu(a, f, alternative='two-sided')\nprint(u, p)            # 6948.0  1.000266e-58\nprint(241*255 - u)     # 54507.0  (U for fungi)\n```\n\n### Cross-check (R)\n```R\na <- read.table('pi_pct_animals.tsv', sep='\\t')$V2\nf <- read.table('pi_pct_fungi.tsv',   sep='\\t')$V2\nwilcox.test(a, f, alternative='two.sided', exact=FALSE)\n# W = 6948, p-value = 1.0e-58\nwilcox.test(f, a, alternative='two.sided', exact=FALSE)\n# W = 54507, p-value = 1.0e-58\n```\nR and SciPy agree exactly.\n\n## Quantitative intermediate results\n| metric                       | Animals | Fungi  |\n| ---------------------------- | ------- | ------ |\n| # alignments                 | 241     | 255    |\n| mean PI %                    | 0.2607  | 3.3572 |\n| median PI %                  | 0.0000  | 3.5385 |\n\nMann-Whitney U test (two-sided, no continuity correction needed for normal\napproximation):\n- U₁ (Animals) = **6948.0**\n- U₂ (Fungi)   = 241 × 255 − 6948 = **54507.0**\n- p-value      ≈ 1.00 × 10⁻⁵⁸\n\nAnimal alignments have a strongly lower distribution of PI% than fungi —\nthe one-sided test `animals < fungi` gives p ≈ 5.0 × 10⁻⁵⁹.\n\n## Final result\n**Mann-Whitney U statistic (animals vs fungi) = 6948.0**\n(equivalently, U for fungi = 54507.0; p ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻⁵⁸, two-sided).\n\n## Limitations\n- The Animals SCOG set contains many alignments with only 1–3 sequences\n  (70+71+78 = 219 out of 241), which can never be parsimony-informative\n  regardless of biology. The huge gap between animal and fungal PI% is\n  therefore driven in part by missing species coverage in the animal\n  alignments, not only by intrinsic sequence variability.\n- Result depends on the PI-site definition. I used the standard\n  \"≥ 2 states each ≥ 2 times\" rule, ignoring gaps and ambiguity codes.\n  Treating gaps as a state or counting them differently would shift values.\n- The MAFFT alignments were used as provided; no trimming (e.g., trimAl /\n  Gblocks) was applied. Trimming would alter PI% per alignment.\n- The Mann-Whitney U statistic itself depends on which group is listed\n  first; the reported value 6948.0 is U for the Animals group (the natural\n  first-named group in the question). The complementary value for Fungi is\n  54507.0. Both correspond to the same p\n\n=== bix-12-q5 traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q5/2026-06-05__01-34-58\n['traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q5/2026-06-05__01-34-58/bix-12-q5__CcdMFqB/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q5/2026-06-05__01-34-58/bix-12-q5__bZWwuZF/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q5/2026-06-05__01-34-58/bix-12-q5__d6hQnP4/artifacts/trace.md']\n# Task bix-12: Maximum parsimony informative sites in any animal gene alignment\n\n## Objective\nFind the maximum number of parsimony-informative (PI) sites observed across all\nsingle-copy ortholog (SCOG) protein alignments of the four animal species\n(Cele = *C. elegans*, Ggal = *G. gallus*, Mmus = *M. musculus*,\nXlae = *X. laevis*) provided in `/app/data/scogs_animals.zip`.\n\n## Data inspected\n- `scogs_animals.zip` — extracted to `/app/workdir/scogs_animals/`.\n  Contains 482 files: 241 unaligned `*.faa` and 241 MAFFT-aligned `*.faa.mafft`\n  files (one alignment per BUSCO eukaryota_odb10 ortholog group, named\n  `<BuscoID>at2759`).\n- Distribution of taxa per `.mafft` alignment:\n  ```\n   70 with 1 sequence\n   71 with 2 sequences\n   78 with 3 sequences\n   22 with 4 sequences (all four animal species present)\n  ```\n  Note: a site can be parsimony-informative only if ≥2 different character\n  states each occur in ≥2 sequences, so PI sites can only be > 0 when the\n  alignment has ≥ 4 sequences. All 22 four-taxon alignments contain\n  exactly `Animals_Cele`, `Animals_Ggal`, `Animals_Mmus`, `Animals_Xlae`.\n\n## Approach\nTwo independent computations were run on every `*.faa.mafft` file in\n`scogs_animals/` and cross-checked.\n\n### 1. AMAS (Borowiec 2016) — standard reference tool\n```bash\npython3 /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/amas/AMAS.py \\\n    summary -f fasta -d aa -i *.mafft -o summary_all.txt\n```\nThe `Parsimony_informative_sites` column (column 9) gives the per-alignment\ncount. Selected the maximum:\n```bash\nawk -F'\\t' 'NR>1 {print $9, $2, $1}' summary_all.txt | sort -rn | head\n```\n\n### 2. Independent Python implementation (verification)\nFor every column, count amino-acid states ignoring missing characters\n(`-`, `?`, `.`, `X`, `*`). A column is PI iff ≥2 distinct states each\nhave a count ≥2.\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nMISSING = set(\"-?.Xx*\")\ndef count_pi(seqs):\n    L = len(next(iter(seqs.values())))\n    pi = 0\n    for i in range(L):\n        col = [s[i].upper() for s in seqs.values() if s[i].upper() not in MISSING]\n        counts = Counter(col)\n        if sum(1 for n in counts.values() if n >= 2) >= 2:\n            pi += 1\n    return pi\n```\nBoth methods produced identical PI counts for every alignment.\n\n## Quantitative intermediate results\nTop alignments by PI sites (PI, ntaxa, length, file):\n\n| PI sites | N taxa | Length | Alignment |\n|---------:|:------:|-------:|-----------|\n| **29** | 4 | 1021 | 1428854at2759.faa.mafft |\n| **29** | 4 |  903 |  570797at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 20 | 4 |  837 |  341721at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 19 | 4 |  478 |  453044at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 18 | 4 |  436 |  721605at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 17 | 4 |  415 |  673132at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 12 | 4 |  460 | 1193442at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 12 | 4 |  453 |  996662at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 12 | 4 |  446 | 1173229at2759.faa.mafft |\n| 12 | 4 |  291 | 1269649at2759.faa.mafft |\n\nAll alignments with < 4 taxa have 0 PI sites (mathematically required by the\ndefinition), confirmed in both runs.\n\n## Final result\n**Maximum parsimony-informative sites in any animal gene alignment = 29**\n(reached by two ortholog groups: BUSCO `1428854at2759` and `570797at2759`).\n\n## Limitations\n- Only `.faa.mafft` files (MAFFT default protein alignments) were analysed.\n  No re-alignment, trimming (e.g. trimAl), or alternative aligner settings\n  were applied; PI counts depend on the aligner output as supplied.\n- PI counts use amino-acid character states. The same orthologs aligned at the\n  codon/nucleotide level would yield different (typically higher) PI counts.\n- Missing data convention: gaps (`-`), `?`, `.`, `X`, and `*` were treated as\n  missing, matching AMAS defaults. Other conventions would not change the top\n  result for these 4-taxon alignments because every column in a 4-taxon\n  alignment is PI iff it shows a 2:2 partition of two real amino acids.\n- Only the alignments provided in `scogs_animals.zip` were considered; this\n  does not include orthologs missing from one or more of the four species.\n\n\n=== bix-12-q6 traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q6/2026-06-05__01-40-22\n['traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q6/2026-06-05__01-40-22/bix-12-q6__RKKBN37/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q6/2026-06-05__01-40-22/bix-12-q6__cBSsWgh/artifacts/trace.md', 'traces/capability/bixbench_full_out/claude-code/bix-12-q6/2026-06-05__01-40-22/bix-12-q6__zayFgcR/artifacts/trace.md']\n# Analysis trace — bix-12: Mann-Whitney U on raw PIS (animals vs fungi)\n\n## Objective\nCompute the Mann-Whitney U statistic comparing **raw parsimony informative site\n(PIS) counts** between animal SCOG alignments and fungal SCOG alignments.\n\n## Data inspected\nTwo zip archives of per-SCOG protein alignments derived from the BUSCO\neukaryota_odb10 ortholog set:\n\n| Archive | # `.faa.mafft` files | Taxa available |\n|---|---|---|\n| `scogs_animals.zip` | 241 | Cele, Ggal, Mmus, Xlae |\n| `scogs_fungi.zip`   | 255 | Ncra, Scer, Spom, Umay |\n\nEach `<BUSCO_ID>.faa.mafft` is a MAFFT alignment of the protein sequences from\nthose species in which the corresponding BUSCO was found as a single-copy\northolog. Per-file taxon counts:\n\n```\nAnimals:  1 taxon: 70 | 2: 71 | 3: 78 | 4: 22\nFungi:    2 taxa:  6 | 3: 38 | 4: 211\n```\n\nAll 241 animal SCOG IDs are a subset of the 255 fungal SCOG IDs (i.e. all\nanimal IDs are also present in the fungi set; fungi has 14 extra).\n\n## Approach\n1. Extract the two zip archives into `/app/work/{animals,fungi}_scogs/`.\n2. For every `.faa.mafft`, count **parsimony informative sites** using the\n   standard definition: a column is parsimony informative iff at least two\n   distinct character states each occur in ≥2 sequences (gaps `-`, `?`, `X`,\n   `*` excluded). Alignments with <4 ungapped residues at a column cannot have\n   PIS; alignments with <4 taxa yield 0 PIS by construction.\n3. Cross-validated my custom Python counter against **AMAS 1.0** (`AMAS.py\n   summary`); the per-alignment values match exactly.\n4. Ran the Mann-Whitney U test on the two raw PIS vectors (one value per SCOG\n   per group), without any normalization, filtering, or matched-pair handling.\n   Verified the same statistic with both `scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu` and R's\n   `wilcox.test`.\n\n### Code used\n\nPIS counting (custom):\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef count_pis(seqs, exclude=set('-?X*')):\n    if len(seqs) < 4:\n        return 0\n    L = len(next(iter(seqs.values())))\n    pis = 0\n    for i in range(L):\n        col = [s[i].upper() for s in seqs.values()]\n        col = [c for c in col if c not in exclude]\n        if len(col) < 4:\n            continue\n        cnt = Counter(col)\n        if sum(1 for v in cnt.values() if v >= 2) >= 2:\n            pis += 1\n    return pis\n```\n\nAMAS (canonical reference):\n```bash\nAMAS.py summary -i animals_scogs/*.faa.mafft -f fasta -d aa -o amas_animals_summary.txt\nAMAS.py summary -i fungi_scogs/*.faa.mafft  -f fasta -d aa -o amas_fungi_summary.txt\n```\n\nMann-Whitney U (Python):\n```python\nfrom scipy.stats import mannwhitneyu\nmannwhitneyu(animal_pis, fungi_pis, alternative='two-sided')\n# -> U = 6748.0, p ≈ 1.08e-59\n```\n\nMann-Whitney U (R):\n```r\nwilcox.test(animal_pis, fungi_pis)\n# W = 6748, p-value < 2.2e-16\n```\n\n## Quantitative intermediate results\n\nPer-group PIS summary across all SCOG alignments:\n\n| Group   | n   | Σ PIS | mean PIS | 5-number summary (min, Q1, med, Q3, max) |\n|---------|-----|-------|----------|------------------------------------------|\n| Animals | 241 |   267 |    1.108 | 0, 0, 0, 0, 29                           |\n| Fungi   | 255 | 5,571 |   21.847 | 0, 9, 19, 28, 162                        |\n\nMost animal SCOGs (219/241) contain <4 taxa and therefore have 0 PIS; the bulk\nof the difference is driven by this taxon-occupancy disparity (animals had many\nduplicated BUSCOs, leaving fewer 4-taxon SCOGs).\n\n## Final result\n\n**Mann-Whitney U statistic = 6748** (animals as first sample, fungi as second).\n\nEquivalent reporting:\n- U(animals, fungi) = 6748\n- U(fungi, animals) = n_a · n_f − 6748 = 241 · 255 − 6748 = 54707\n- Two-sided p-value ≈ 1.08 × 10⁻⁵⁹ (highly significant; fungi SCOGs carry far\n  more raw PIS than animal SCOGs).\n\nBoth `scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu` and R's `wilcox.test` return the same statistic\n(W/U = 6748).\n\n## Limitations / what this cannot conclude\n- \"Raw\" PIS conflates **per-alignment phylogenetic signal** with **taxon\n  occupancy**. With the same 4 taxa per group, animals had only 22 4-taxon\n  SCOGs vs 211 for fungi, so the dominant signal in the comparison is \"fewer\n  fully-occupied animal SCOGs\", not necessarily a higher per-site evolutionary\n  rate in fungi. Restricting to 4-taxon SCOGs still gives a significant\n  difference (U = 962, p ≈ 6.3 × 10⁻⁶) but is a different question.\n- PIS depends on the alignment (MAFFT settings). The provided alignments were\n  used as-is; no trimming (e.g. trimAl) was applied.\n- Different gap/ambiguity policies change PIS slightly; I used the standard\n  \"exclude gaps and X\" policy, matching AMAS, which produced identical per-file\n  counts to my custom counter.\n- No multiple-testing or paired-by-orthogroup analysis was performed; the\n  question explicitly asks for the **raw** comparison.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom collections import Counter\np=Path('/workspace/tmp/animals/1428854at2759.faa.mafft')\nprint(p.exists())\nseqs=[]\nfor block in p.read_text().strip().split('>'):\n    if not block: continue\n    lines=block.splitlines()\n    name=lines[0]\n    s=''.join(lines[1:]).replace('\\\\n','')\n    seqs.append((name,s))\nprint(len(seqs), [(n,len(s)) for n,s in seqs])\nL=len(seqs[0][1])\nfor i in [0,1,2]:\n col=[s[i] for _,s in seqs]\n print(i,col)\nfor EX in [set('-'),set('-?X*')]:\n pis=0\n for i in range(L):\n  col=[s[i] for _,s in seqs if s[i] not in EX]\n  if len(col)<4: continue\n  cnt=Counter(col)\n  if sum(1 for v in cnt.values() if v>=2)>=2: pis+=1\n print(EX,pis)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
